<Read title="Astrology around the world: Western, Indian, Chinese, Maya and more — Svapna" description="How astrology differs by region: the Western tropical zodiac, India’s sidereal zodiac and moon stations, China’s twelve animals and Four Pillars, the Maya 260-day count, and traditions in Africa, Southeast Asia and Tibet." path="/stars/traditions" image="/og/stars.jpg" crumbs={[{ name: 'The sky', path: '/stars' }, { name: 'Around the world', path: '/stars/traditions' }]}>
  <p class="small crumb"><a href="/">Svapna</a> · <a href="/stars">The sky</a> · Around the world</p>
  <h1>One sky, <em>read differently.</em></h1>
  <p class="lede">There are three great families of astrology. One grew from Babylon and now covers Europe, the Americas, the Islamic world and India. One grew in China and covers East Asia. One grew in Mesoamerica and is still kept in the highlands of Guatemala. They start from different things: the first from where the planets are, the second from the calendar, the third from a count of days.</p>
  <StarsNav path="/stars/traditions" />

  <div class="prose">
    <h2>Europe and the Americas: the Western zodiac</h2>
    <p>Western astrology reads a chart of the sky for the minute and place of birth. The chart shows the sun, the moon and the planets in the twelve signs, and in twelve houses that stand for areas of life such as work, money and marriage. The sign rising in the east at birth, the ascendant, is counted as important as the sun’s.</p>
    <p>Most people know only their sun sign, which depends on the birthday alone. That habit dates from the newspaper columns of the 1930s. A practising astrologer treats the sun sign as one item in about forty.</p>
    <p>Since the early twentieth century Western astrology has mostly described character. It took on the language of psychology, and many of its practitioners now say that it describes tendencies and leave prediction aside.</p>

    <h2 id="two-zodiacs">India: Jyotisha, and the second zodiac</h2>
    <p>Indian astrology, called Jyotisha, “the science of lights”, uses the same twelve signs under Sanskrit names, taken over from the Greeks in the first centuries CE. It differs from the Western kind in three ways.</p>
    <p>It measures the zodiac from the fixed stars. Western astrology measures from the point where the sun stands at the spring equinox, and that point slips backward against the stars by one degree in about seventy-two years. Two thousand years ago the two zodiacs agreed. They are now about twenty-four degrees apart, which is most of a sign, so a person who is a Leo in a Western chart usually has the sun in Cancer in an Indian one.</p>
    <p>It gives more weight to the moon than to the sun. A person’s <em>rashi</em> is the sign the moon was in. The moon’s path is also divided into twenty-seven <em>nakshatras</em>, stations of thirteen degrees and twenty minutes each, an older Indian system found in the Vedas. The birth nakshatra is used to choose the first syllable of a child’s name in many families.</p>
    <p>And it predicts timing. A system of planetary periods called <em>dashas</em>, counted from the moon’s position at birth, divides a life into stretches of years ruled by each planet in turn.</p>
    <p>Jyotisha is part of ordinary life in India to a degree with no Western parallel. Families compare the charts of a prospective bride and groom, a practice called matching <em>kundlis</em>, and priests choose the <em>muhurta</em>, the favourable hour, for weddings, journeys and the opening of a business. In 2001 India’s University Grants Commission approved university courses in Jyotisha, and the Supreme Court upheld the decision in 2004 against a challenge by scientists.</p>

    <h2>China and East Asia: the calendar as the chart</h2>
    <p>Chinese astrology starts from the calendar. Years, months, days and two-hour periods are each named by pairing one of ten “heavenly stems” with one of twelve “earthly branches”, which gives a cycle of sixty. The cycle is used to count days on the oracle bones of the Shang dynasty, more than three thousand years ago.</p>
    <p>The twelve animals, rat through pig, are popular names for the twelve branches. The earliest lists are on bamboo slips from tombs of the third century BCE. The ten stems carry the five phases (wood, fire, earth, metal, water), so each year has an animal and a phase: 2026 is a year of the Fire Horse.</p>
    <p>A full reading uses the stem and branch of the year, month, day and hour of birth: eight characters in four pairs, called <em>bazi</em> or the Four Pillars of Destiny. The method took its present form in the Tang and Song dynasties, between the eighth and eleventh centuries. The reader weighs the balance of the five phases among the eight characters. The planets hardly come into it.</p>
    <p>For most of Chinese history the sky itself was the emperor’s business. An imperial bureau watched for eclipses, comets and “guest stars” as signs about the dynasty, and private study of such omens was at times forbidden. The sky was divided into twenty-eight lunar lodges where the West had twelve signs.</p>
    <p>The system spread to Korea, where the Four Pillars are called <em>saju</em>, to Vietnam, where the rabbit is a cat and the ox a water buffalo, and to Japan. In Japan a belief that women born in a Fire Horse year make dangerous wives was strong enough that births fell by about a quarter in 1966, the last such year before 2026.</p>

    <h2>Tibet, and Southeast Asia</h2>
    <p>Tibetan astrology combines the two great Asian systems. “White” calculation, <em>kartsi</em>, came from India with the Kalachakra teachings in the eleventh century and deals with planets and the calendar. “Black” calculation, <em>naktsi</em>, came from China and uses the animals and the five phases. Tibetan almanacs are compiled from both.</p>
    <p>In Burma the day of the week on which a person was born matters most. Each day has a planet, a compass direction and an animal, and Wednesday is split in two to make eight. Pagodas have a shrine for each, and people pour water at their own. In Thailand each day of the week has a colour, and the birth day shapes the choice of a name.</p>

    <h2>The Islamic world, and Africa</h2>
    <p>Astrologers writing in Arabic and Persian between the eighth and the thirteenth centuries preserved the Greek system, added Indian and Persian methods, and passed the whole to Europe. Religious scholars mostly rejected the prediction of events as a claim to knowledge that belongs to God, while accepting the astronomy needed for the calendar and the times of prayer. Both attitudes continue.</p>
    <p>Egypt contributed the decans: thirty-six groups of stars whose risings marked the hours of the night, painted inside coffin lids from about 2100 BCE. The round zodiac on the ceiling of the temple at Dendera, from about 50 BCE, shows them together with the twelve Babylonian signs.</p>
    <p>Arabic astrology and the related art of sand divination travelled with Islam across the Sahara and down the East African coast, and manuscripts on both are among those kept at Timbuktu. In Madagascar the months of destiny, <em>vintana</em>, carry names taken from the Arabic names of the zodiac signs, and a diviner uses them to choose days for a house-building, a burial or a wedding.</p>

    <h2>Mesoamerica: a count of days</h2>
    <p>The Maya, the Aztecs and their neighbours kept a calendar of 260 days, made by running twenty named days against the numbers one to thirteen. Each of the 260 days has its own character. A child’s birth day gave its nature and prospects, and among the Aztecs often its name. Specialists read the days from painted books; the Spanish friar Bernardino de Sahagún recorded their readings in the sixteenth century.</p>
    <p>The Maya also tracked Venus closely. A table in the Dresden Codex predicts its appearances as morning and evening star over centuries, and its first rising was a time of danger.</p>
    <p>The 260-day count has never stopped. In the highlands of Guatemala, K’iche’ Maya daykeepers still keep it, without a break since before the Spanish arrived, and people consult them about births, illnesses and decisions.</p>

    <h2>The Pacific, and elsewhere</h2>
    <p>Every people has knowledge of the sky. Polynesian navigators steered by the rising points of stars, and the rising of the Pleiades, Matariki, marks the Māori new year. Aboriginal Australians read the seasons from the stars and saw shapes in the dark lanes of the Milky Way. This is astronomy, calendar and story. Reading a person’s character from the moment of birth is a narrower thing, and it belongs to the three families described above.</p>

    <h2>The systems side by side</h2>
    <div class="steps">
      <div><b>Western</b><div><p>Begins from the planets’ places at the minute of birth. Zodiac measured from the equinox. Best-known item: the sun sign. Mostly describes character.</p></div></div>
      <div><b>Indian</b><div><p>Begins from the same chart, with the zodiac measured from the stars. Best-known item: the moon sign and birth nakshatra. Describes character and times events.</p></div></div>
      <div><b>Chinese</b><div><p>Begins from the calendar: the year, month, day and hour of birth. Best-known item: the animal of the year. Weighs the balance of five phases.</p></div></div>
      <div><b>Maya</b><div><p>Begins from a 260-day count. Best-known item: the day sign and its number. Each day has a character, read by a daykeeper.</p></div></div>
    </div>
    <p>The traditions give different answers for the same person, and each is consistent by its own rules. <a href="/stars/your-signs">Enter a birth date</a> to see five of them for one day.</p>

